fix(pip): Map the lens as a pure projection difference, and fix the heat vision element index

The self calibrating diagnostic settled the projection question with measurements instead of
another guess: both readings of the composed matrix reported a sampled span of 0 for
magnifications of 3 and 1.2, so the mapping was degenerate and the lens displayed a single texel
stretched over the aperture - the fully black lens that does not change with direction.

The composition was the problem, not the convention. The lens camera is the game camera with a
magnified projection, so the two passes share the view matrix by construction and the mapping
between them is a pure projection difference: captured_ndc = P_lens * inv(P_main) * main_ndc. The
view matrices cancel, and with them the operand order and upload convention questions that had
been producing wrong spans (1.5 and 0.62 then 0.0). The published matrix is now exactly that
difference and the binder uses it as is.

Also found while digging into the thermal render: CBlender_heatvision compiles a SINGLE pass, so
s_heatvision only has E[0], while phase_heatvision selected E[ps_r2_heatvision] - with the switch
at 1 (any heat vision device, the pack's script included) that is an out of range element and a
black screen. That is a pre existing engine bug, not a fork regression, and it is why forcing
r__heatvision went black. The pass now selects E[0].

phase_combine also runs the heat pass for the lens pass when the optic is a thermal one
(svp_optic_thermal or image type >= 2), which needs no console switch: the heat vision shader
derives its image from the position buffer and the combined image, and the lens set has its own
copies of both. That is the per lens thermal render the earlier attempt was trying to reach by
flipping a frame level switch.